     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">I am using ddply (from plyr),</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">and I have a question.</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">I have a function which needs 2 matrices as argument</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">fun(mat1,mat2)</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">With a dataframe mydf, I want to use ddply in subsets, and the matrices are constructed by cbinding variables on the dataframe</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">Computing the result for the whole dataframe would be done by</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">with(mydf,fun(cbind(vara1,vara2),cbind(varb1,varb2)))</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">I can do this using ddply for groups.</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">But there is an additional complication:</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">I want to construct the matrices from many variables, namely 80, and I would rather avoid typing all 80 variable names.</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">For the whole dataframe, I can do</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">fun(as.matrix(subset(mydf,select=vara1:vara5)),as.matrix(subset(mydf,select=varb1:varab80)))</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">is there a way of working of matrices constructed by cbinding variables from the dataframe on the</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">subsets of dataframes created by the slicing process of ddply?</span><br style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">I am currently doing something like</span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\"><br></span></div><div><div>fun(cbind(vara1,vara2),</div><div>      eval(parse(text=paste(&quot;cbind(&quot;,paste(<span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);font-family:Helvetica;font-size:12px\">paste(&quot;varb&quot;,1:80,&quot;,&quot;)</span>,collapse=&quot;,&quot;),&quot;)&quot;))))</div></div><div><br></div><div>which works but is not really easily readable.</div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div><br></div></div>"
